WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our next guest
is an Emmy-winning journalist

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.870
who hosts "This Week
with George Stephanopoulos"

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.707
Sunday mornings and co-anchors
"Good Morning America"

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:09.075
weekdays on ABC.

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:11.844
Please welcome back to the show
George Stephanopoulos.

00:00:11.844 --> 00:00:13.012
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:22.589 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:24.357
-Welcome to the show.
-Good to be back.

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:25.925
-You have a congratulations
in order.

00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:27.894
You just celebrated
a 20th wedding anniversary

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:30.496
with Ali Wentworth.
-Yes.

00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:32.799
I'm very lucky.
-Fantastic comedian, writer.

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:34.867
-Did you you do anything special
for your 20th?

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:37.236
-We actually didn't, no.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:37.236 --> 00:00:40.006
It was one of those things
where we both had to go visit

00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:42.175
our parents that weekend.
-Oh, that's very nice.

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:44.143
-Yeah, it was just something
that had to happen,

00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:46.779
but we had
a little celebration at home.

00:00:46.779 --> 00:00:48.614
And we -- you know --
I don't know

00:00:48.614 --> 00:00:51.484
how long you've been married.
-I don't know, either.

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:53.819
[ Laughter ]

00:00:53.819 --> 00:00:55.588
-You know, everybody tells you,
"Oh, you're gonna blink,

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:57.123
and it's gonna go by."
-Yeah.

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:59.926
-It's so true.
I cannot believe

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:01.193
we've been together 20 years,

00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:02.995
but it's been the happiest
20 years of my life.

00:01:02.995 --> 00:01:06.266
-Well, congratulations on that.
[ Applause ]

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:08.134
I have a lot of questions
for you about your work,

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:11.571
but I want to start asking,
what time does your day start

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.205
hosting a morning show
the way you do?

00:01:13.205 --> 00:01:15.308
-I get up at 2:30.
-2:30 in the morning.

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:17.009
-So this is nighttime for me.
-Yeah.

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:19.845
So you could
set your alarm a little earlier

00:01:19.845 --> 00:01:22.715
and watch this when it airs.
-Exactly.

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:24.517
-So 2:30 in the morning,
you get up.

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:26.085
and is that
just for the purposes --

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:29.155
-It's the way my body works.
You know, I get up, I meditate.

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:30.590
And, you know, 7:00 a.m. --

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.425
We start to do some stuff
at 6:00.

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:33.926
7:00 a.m. has to be
the middle of my day.

00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:36.095
-Sure. It can't be like
I'm rolling out of bed, so...

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:38.665
-So how often can you
actually go out

00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:41.133
and do something in the evening?
-Almost never.

00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:42.401
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:42.401 --> 00:01:44.637
So for your 20th anniversary,
you had a brunch.

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:47.740
[ Laughter ]
You had a romantic brunch.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.611
-Early-bird special.
-I want to ask you about

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:54.580
a recent interview you did that
received a lot of attention.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:55.915
You interviewed Alec Baldwin.

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:58.217
You were the first person
to interview him on camera.

00:01:58.217 --> 00:02:01.020
I can only imagine
how stressful it must be

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.255
to talk to someone
who has gone through

00:02:03.255 --> 00:02:05.524
the traumatic experience
that he just went through.

00:02:05.524 --> 00:02:08.828
-It was crazy, you know.
I've probably done, like you,

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:11.664
thousands of interviews
over the last 20 years.

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:14.734
I had never had
an experience this intense.

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:18.437
He walked in by himself.
The entire crew went silent.

00:02:18.437 --> 00:02:22.942
And for the next hour,
it was just minute by minute,

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:26.846
gripping emotion from him.

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:28.348
And it's all so close
to the surface.

00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:31.818
I mean, he's described it as
a one-in-a-trillion accident.

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:33.686
It was
a one-in-a-trillion accident,

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:37.990
but he was holding the gun,
and his cinematographer is dead.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.092
"How do you live with that?"
is something --

00:02:40.092 --> 00:02:42.996
You know, that was
the big question I had for him.

00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:46.099
And it was so clear that this
was all so close to the surface.

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:49.936
I think he probably wasn't
sitting down in the chair

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:55.508
for a minute and a half before
he started getting emotional.

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:57.776
And the entire hour
was like that.

00:02:57.776 --> 00:03:00.380
But to his credit,
he answered every question,

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.282
went through the entire day
with what happened.

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:05.351
The investigation
is still going on.

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:08.821
I think he just wanted
to come out and to say

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:12.424
his side of the story
and say how badly he felt.

00:03:12.424 --> 00:03:16.429
-It's very wonderful
when you can provide

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:18.197
this sort of storytelling
of the things

00:03:18.197 --> 00:03:20.432
that everybody
is thinking about.

00:03:20.432 --> 00:03:23.736
And you're doing that, as well,
not just with that interview,

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:26.439
which I think everybody wanted
to hear what Alec had to say.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:28.908
You have a couple of shows
on Hulu right now.

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:30.309
One is about Christopher Steele.

00:03:30.309 --> 00:03:32.945
And obviously much has been
written about his dossier.

00:03:32.945 --> 00:03:35.481
How are we to take
Christopher Steele right now,

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:38.817
based on the fact that obviously
there was a very partisan split

00:03:38.817 --> 00:03:41.186
as far as how
he was evaluated at first,

00:03:41.186 --> 00:03:43.323
and now where -- how would you
hold him as a journalist?

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:45.725
-That's a great question.
And we talked it through

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:47.560
with my team for hours and hours

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.228
both before and after
the interview.

00:03:49.228 --> 00:03:51.598
I think, you look at
the broad outlines,

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:55.067
he was basically writing about
Russian contacts with Trump

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:58.804
and the Trump campaign in 2016.

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:02.508
Robert Mueller, you know,
sort of verified most of that.

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:04.644
On the other hand,
there were some other things

00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:07.413
in the dossier,
like the famous pee tape,

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:08.948
like the idea
that Michael Cohen,

00:04:08.948 --> 00:04:11.183
who was Trump's lawyer,
went to Prague to work out

00:04:11.183 --> 00:04:15.020
some deal with the Russians,
that there is zero evidence

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:16.589
that has come forward
to back that up.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:19.725
And, in fact, Mueller even said
that he, more or less,

00:04:19.725 --> 00:04:23.029
could disprove
the Michael Cohen stuff.

00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:25.598
So he was wrong on the most
sensational things,

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:28.434
or hasn't been able to verify
the most sensational things.

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:31.537
But in the broad outline,
this stuff did happen.

00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:33.372
There were contacts
between Russia

00:04:33.372 --> 00:04:35.841
and the Trump campaign in 2016.

00:04:35.841 --> 00:04:38.811
And I think that gets lost
in some of the coverage

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:42.615
of Christopher Steele right now,
because on these big things

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:45.451
that got so much attention,
he hasn't been able to prove it.

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:47.653
-And it does seem like
some journalists

00:04:47.653 --> 00:04:49.388
are having to go back
and reflect on the fact

00:04:49.388 --> 00:04:51.624
that maybe they sort of
ran to the Steele dossier

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:54.226
a little bit faster --
-Did the people really do that?

00:04:54.226 --> 00:04:55.527
I mean the Steele dossier --
-I guess that's my question.

00:04:55.527 --> 00:04:57.396
-It didn't come out
during the campaign.

00:04:57.396 --> 00:04:59.832
It came out after the campaign.
-Sure, but it did seem like,

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:01.500
perhaps, it made good television

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.403
to talk about
the Steele dossier.

00:05:03.403 --> 00:05:05.838
-Right. And a lot of people did.
A lot of people went too far.

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:09.942
I think we at ABC
were very careful with it.

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:13.112
We didn't sensationalize
the claims in any way,

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:15.314
and we didn't report
what we couldn't verify.

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:17.283
But I think
some did take it too far.

00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:20.052
-You also tell a story,
another Hulu show --

00:05:20.052 --> 00:05:21.386
this is about someone
who stopped

00:05:21.386 --> 00:05:24.991
a homegrown terrorist attack.
And are you surprised,

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:26.925
or, were you surprised
how little

00:05:26.925 --> 00:05:29.061
people knew about this story?
-This is an amazing story.

00:05:29.061 --> 00:05:33.266
This guy is a guy named Dan Day,
Garden City, Kansas.

00:05:33.266 --> 00:05:36.335
Unemployed security guard,
average guy, two kids,

00:05:36.335 --> 00:05:39.171
50-something years old.

00:05:39.171 --> 00:05:41.774
He's kind of a Trumper,
kind of a conservative.

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:45.578
In 2015, he fell in
with some white supremacists.

00:05:45.578 --> 00:05:47.012
He was horrified by what he saw,

00:05:47.012 --> 00:05:48.981
and he became an informant
for the FBI.

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:52.284
Was an FBI informant
for 15 months

00:05:52.284 --> 00:05:55.321
as these people started to plan
what would have been

00:05:55.321 --> 00:05:57.390
the deadliest terror attack
in American history --

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:59.091
domestic terror attack
in American history.

00:05:59.091 --> 00:06:01.227
They were gonna blow up
the entire Somali community

00:06:01.227 --> 00:06:02.628
in Garden City, Kansas.

00:06:02.628 --> 00:06:06.065
We have the tapes that are
in the documentary about that.

00:06:06.065 --> 00:06:08.334
It didn't get
all that much attention

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:10.736
because the plot was foiled

00:06:10.736 --> 00:06:13.806
three weeks
before the 2016 election.

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:16.509
The bombs were supposed
to go off the day after

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:19.412
the 2016 election,
because they didn't want

00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:22.347
to help Hillary Clinton
and all of that.

00:06:22.347 --> 00:06:27.519
But this story, this man
was so heroic, so selfless

00:06:27.519 --> 00:06:31.991
and put himself and his family
at risk to stop

00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:33.592
what would've been one of
the most horrifying things

00:06:33.592 --> 00:06:35.494
you'd ever see.
-When you talk to someone

00:06:35.494 --> 00:06:38.397
like that, do you get the sense
that they understand now

00:06:38.397 --> 00:06:41.267
their own heroism?

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:43.068
-Every once in a while
on "Good Morning America,"

00:06:43.068 --> 00:06:46.239
we meet people, as well, who
save people from a burning fire,

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:48.507
from a shark
or something like that.

00:06:48.507 --> 00:06:51.877
To a person, they always say,
"No, I'm not a hero."

00:06:51.877 --> 00:06:56.682
And when any person looking
at what they did would say,

00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:58.350
"I'm not sure
I could've done that.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:00.252
I'm not sure I could've
put my family at risk.

00:07:00.252 --> 00:07:02.789
I'm not sure
I could've been hanging out

00:07:02.789 --> 00:07:05.323
with these violent people
who were gonna do

00:07:05.323 --> 00:07:09.461
horrible things and maybe even
put a bullet in my head,"

00:07:09.461 --> 00:07:14.033
and he just kept on persisting
in order to stop it.

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:16.335
To this day, he doesn't believe
he's a hero.

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:17.403
He certainly is.

00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:19.872
-I would agree with that,
as well.

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:23.075
Can I ask, you know,
those must be assignments

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:25.877
that any journalist
would look forward to,

00:07:25.877 --> 00:07:27.646
getting to talk to a hero
like that, telling his story,

00:07:27.646 --> 00:07:29.581
getting to talk
to Christopher Steele.

00:07:29.581 --> 00:07:31.884
What is the worst assignment
you've ever been given?

00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:33.619
[ Laughter ]

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:37.422
One of the best April Fools
jokes my wife ever played on me,

00:07:37.422 --> 00:07:40.759
it was years and years ago
on "Good Morning America."

00:07:40.759 --> 00:07:42.261
You know,
you often have to promote things

00:07:42.261 --> 00:07:44.163
on this show, as well.
-Yeah.

00:07:44.163 --> 00:07:46.466
-And I was in
the middle of the show.

00:07:46.466 --> 00:07:48.167
And she got
the executive producers

00:07:48.167 --> 00:07:50.403
to come into my ear and say,
"You have to fly to L.A.

00:07:50.403 --> 00:07:53.905
right after the show
and go interview the cast

00:07:53.905 --> 00:07:55.641
of some show that was definitely
gonna be cancelled

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:58.644
three weeks later."
[ Laughter ]

00:07:58.644 --> 00:08:03.249
And she saw me
starting to turn red on set.

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:05.751
The entire crew was watching,
saying,

00:08:05.751 --> 00:08:07.152
"You got to take him
out of his misery.

00:08:07.152 --> 00:08:09.922
He's gonna blow up on air."
[ Laughter ]

00:08:09.922 --> 00:08:12.157
They saved me.
-That was very nice.

00:08:12.157 --> 00:08:14.526
Your "GMA" colleague,
Michael Strahan,

00:08:14.526 --> 00:08:15.828
is gonna go to space.
-Yeah.

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:17.963
It's been postponed, I guess,
for a couple days.

00:08:17.963 --> 00:08:19.531
-Really?
-It was supposed to be tomorrow.

00:08:19.531 --> 00:08:20.833
But there's some weather issue,
but it's gonna

00:08:20.833 --> 00:08:22.668
probably happen this weekend.

00:08:22.668 --> 00:08:24.903
And he's ready to go.
-Would you go?

00:08:24.903 --> 00:08:26.238
-No.
-Nor would I.

00:08:26.238 --> 00:08:28.407
[ Laughter ]
Nor would I.

00:08:28.407 --> 00:08:30.375
-But glad he is.
Not even tempted.

00:08:30.375 --> 00:08:32.244
Not even a little bit.
-Not even a little bit.

00:08:32.244 --> 00:08:33.646
Because you know what?
They got cameras up there,

00:08:33.646 --> 00:08:35.147
so I know what it looks like.
Yeah.

00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:36.649
-It's fine, yeah.
-They're like,

00:08:36.649 --> 00:08:37.749
"You can't believe
what it looks like."

00:08:37.749 --> 00:08:38.817
It's like,
"You took a picture.

00:08:38.817 --> 00:08:40.152
I know exactly
what it looks like."

00:08:40.152 --> 00:08:42.454
-And we're gonna broadcast it,
and all of you can watch.

00:08:42.454 --> 00:08:43.755
Good bless you, Michael.

00:08:43.755 --> 00:08:45.157
-God bless you.
I'm glad he's doing it.

00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:46.425
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:08:46.425 --> 00:08:48.193
Always a pleasure, George.
George Stephanopoulos.

00:08:48.193 --> 00:08:49.328
"This Week
with George Stephanopoulos"

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:50.730
airs Sunday mornings on ABC.

